WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.172
- Oracle is a very competitive company

00:00:02.172 --> 00:00:04.148
and I think there are a
lot of competitive people

00:00:04.148 --> 00:00:06.830
at Oracle, and I enjoy competition.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.747
Don't simply conform to

00:00:10.794 --> 00:00:12.809
conventional ways of thinking.

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:14.809
Translating a good idea,

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.271
into a great product,

00:00:18.271 --> 00:00:20.611
is unbelievably hard.

00:00:20.611 --> 00:00:22.273
We probably spend less money on marketing

00:00:22.273 --> 00:00:24.245
than any company our size in the world.

00:00:24.245 --> 00:00:26.276
We reached a billion dollars in revenue

00:00:26.276 --> 00:00:28.449
and we were having serious
management problems

00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:29.569
all over the place.

00:00:29.569 --> 00:00:31.780
Our goal was to get to 50 employees,

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:35.019
and the goal really was not so
much to make a lot of money.

00:00:35.019 --> 00:00:37.352
My sister once said to me
that whenever I got too close

00:00:37.352 --> 00:00:39.558
to one of my goals, I immediately moved

00:00:39.558 --> 00:00:40.997
the goal post further away.

00:00:40.997 --> 00:00:43.130
So your question was are we a loser?

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:43.963
No!

00:00:43.963 --> 00:00:46.256
If you do everything everyone
else does in business,

00:00:46.256 --> 00:00:47.089
you're going to lose.

00:00:47.089 --> 00:00:49.139
What do you thing Google runs on?

00:00:49.139 --> 00:00:51.189
Did they run on water vapor?

00:00:51.189 --> 00:00:53.434
- He's an American entrepreneur,

00:00:53.434 --> 00:00:55.335
business man, and philanthropist.

00:00:55.335 --> 00:00:57.208
He serves as the Executive Chairman

00:00:57.208 --> 00:00:59.669
and Chief Technology Officer of Oracle.

00:00:59.669 --> 00:01:02.086
As of April 2016, Forbes estimated

00:01:02.086 --> 00:01:04.919
his net worth to be $50.2 billion.

00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:07.828
He's Larry Ellison, and here are his

00:01:07.828 --> 00:01:09.911
Top 10 Rules for Success.

00:01:12.948 --> 00:01:14.725
- Oracle is a very competitive company,

00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:17.101
and I think there are a lot of
competitive people at Oracle.

00:01:17.101 --> 00:01:18.768
I enjoy competition,

00:01:19.748 --> 00:01:21.248
I think life is...

00:01:25.882 --> 00:01:28.840
Kind of a series of acts of discovery.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:33.553
And, I think we're all
interested in our limits,

00:01:33.553 --> 00:01:35.382
what we accomplish in life,

00:01:35.382 --> 00:01:36.885
and discovering our own limits,

00:01:36.885 --> 00:01:38.778
and we might find that in sport,

00:01:38.778 --> 00:01:40.606
we might find that in business.

00:01:40.606 --> 00:01:45.018
I think we're always, you
know looking, you know,

00:01:45.018 --> 00:01:47.220
to discover new things, and new things

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.309
about each other, and new
things about ourselves.

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:51.721
Think things of for yourself,
come to your own judgements.

00:01:51.721 --> 00:01:53.388
Don't simply conform

00:01:55.279 --> 00:01:57.450
to conventional ways of thinking,

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.627
conventional ways of dressing,

00:01:59.627 --> 00:02:01.544
conventional ways of acting.

00:02:01.544 --> 00:02:04.193
That a lot of this, a lot of things are

00:02:04.193 --> 00:02:07.040
based on fashion, even morality at times

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.682
is based on fashion.

00:02:08.682 --> 00:02:11.266
It was once, slavery
was once not considered

00:02:11.266 --> 00:02:12.766
not to be immoral.

00:02:13.857 --> 00:02:17.605
People are shocked that the ancient Greeks

00:02:17.605 --> 00:02:21.231
had slaves, that we had
slavery in this country

00:02:21.231 --> 00:02:24.097
as recently as 130, 140 years ago.

00:02:24.097 --> 00:02:26.489
So, there are moral value, you have to

00:02:26.489 --> 00:02:28.010
really go back to first principles

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.726
and think things out for yourself,

00:02:29.726 --> 00:02:31.168
whether they're scientific principles

00:02:31.168 --> 00:02:33.524
or moral principles, or business ideas,

00:02:33.524 --> 00:02:35.696
or product ideas, you have to think things

00:02:35.696 --> 00:02:36.771
out for yourself.

00:02:36.771 --> 00:02:40.316
Ideas, good ideas, there
are a lot of good ideas.

00:02:40.316 --> 00:02:41.781
- There are a lot of
good ideas in this room?

00:02:41.781 --> 00:02:43.648
- There are a lot of good ideas.

00:02:43.648 --> 00:02:45.648
Translating a good idea,

00:02:46.962 --> 00:02:49.052
into a great product,

00:02:49.052 --> 00:02:50.802
is unbelievably hard.

00:02:52.686 --> 00:02:53.969
There are so many details.

00:02:53.969 --> 00:02:56.732
People accuse Steve of
stealing the Macintosh

00:02:56.732 --> 00:02:58.909
from Xerox with the Alto computer.

00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:01.428
I was at Xerox PARC,
I used the Alto a lot.

00:03:01.428 --> 00:03:06.414
Finishing the Alto, and turning
that into the Macintosh,

00:03:06.414 --> 00:03:10.592
was enormously complicated,
there were so many things

00:03:10.592 --> 00:03:13.517
that had to be done
that Xerox had not done.

00:03:13.517 --> 00:03:14.899
There were a few good ideas,

00:03:14.899 --> 00:03:16.492
in the, in the Xerox machine.

00:03:16.492 --> 00:03:19.514
But Steve would translate good ideas,

00:03:19.514 --> 00:03:21.517
not always his good ideas,

00:03:21.517 --> 00:03:26.016
but he would translate good
ideas into a finished product,

00:03:26.016 --> 00:03:28.007
unlike anyone in our industry.

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:30.648
You'd have to admit, Henry
Ford didn't invent the car,

00:03:30.648 --> 00:03:32.883
but he made it cheap
and he made it popular

00:03:32.883 --> 00:03:35.199
and he made it accessible
to the American people.

00:03:35.199 --> 00:03:37.004
Steve would translate good ideas

00:03:37.004 --> 00:03:40.335
into finished brilliant products.

00:03:40.335 --> 00:03:42.165
We probably spend less money on marketing

00:03:42.165 --> 00:03:45.087
than any company our size in the world.

00:03:45.087 --> 00:03:47.327
We have a tiny marketing department,

00:03:47.327 --> 00:03:50.497
and everyone says, Oracle's
all about sales and marketing.

00:03:50.497 --> 00:03:53.330
We are the largest employer of MIT

00:03:54.235 --> 00:03:56.615
and Cal Tech engineers in the world.

00:03:56.615 --> 00:03:58.421
We are the largest employer of Stanford,

00:03:58.421 --> 00:04:02.914
and Harvard, and CMU
mathematicians in the world.

00:04:02.914 --> 00:04:07.081
We are overwhelmingly dominated
by engineering at Oracle.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.398
The idea we are a sales
and marketing company

00:04:10.398 --> 00:04:13.063
which people will sometimes
write about quite often,

00:04:13.063 --> 00:04:16.227
is ludicrous on the face of it,

00:04:16.227 --> 00:04:20.394
because all of our customers
who buy Oracle already have it.

00:04:21.654 --> 00:04:24.278
And if you already have
Oracle, there's nothing

00:04:24.278 --> 00:04:26.916
a salesman can tell you
about how great it is

00:04:26.916 --> 00:04:29.195
to make you buy more.

00:04:29.195 --> 00:04:32.339
GE already uses it, the Defense
Department already uses it,

00:04:32.339 --> 00:04:35.551
all of our customers,
98% of our sales or more,

00:04:35.551 --> 00:04:38.884
are to existing Oracle customers.

00:04:38.884 --> 00:04:41.072
If we don't have the right product,

00:04:41.072 --> 00:04:43.231
people don't buy more.

00:04:43.231 --> 00:04:45.344
We will, before the acquisition,

00:04:45.344 --> 00:04:49.219
we spent three billion a
year in software engineering.

00:04:49.219 --> 00:04:51.901
The company is all about engineering.

00:04:51.901 --> 00:04:55.111
It's the only thing that works.

00:04:55.111 --> 00:04:56.429
Suddenly we hit a wall,

00:04:56.429 --> 00:04:58.217
we reached a billion dollars in revenue

00:04:58.217 --> 00:05:00.754
and we were having serious
management problems

00:05:00.754 --> 00:05:02.439
all over the place.

00:05:02.439 --> 00:05:05.444
And, the people who were
running the company,

00:05:05.444 --> 00:05:07.231
the billion dollar company,
were the same people

00:05:07.231 --> 00:05:08.128
that would run the company

00:05:08.128 --> 00:05:11.717
when we were a $50 million
company, 1/20th the size.

00:05:11.717 --> 00:05:16.373
And I had an incredible sense
of loyalty to those people

00:05:16.373 --> 00:05:19.376
who worked with me to build Oracle.

00:05:19.376 --> 00:05:23.126
And it was a very painful
realization in 1990

00:05:24.322 --> 00:05:26.738
that I was going to have to
change the management team,

00:05:26.738 --> 00:05:30.488
that the company had
outgrown the management.

00:05:31.862 --> 00:05:34.074
People who are good at
running a $50 million company

00:05:34.074 --> 00:05:36.611
are not necessarily, those
aren't the same skills.

00:05:36.611 --> 00:05:38.968
They're just different,
not one is better or worse,

00:05:38.968 --> 00:05:40.919
they're just entirely different skillset,

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:42.405
in running a $50 million company

00:05:42.405 --> 00:05:45.388
than a billion dollar company.

00:05:45.388 --> 00:05:48.784
And both skillsets are rare and precious,

00:05:48.784 --> 00:05:50.874
but we needed a different
group of managers,

00:05:50.874 --> 00:05:53.335
and virtually, the entire management team

00:05:53.335 --> 00:05:54.810
had to be replaced.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:57.149
That means I had to ask
people who I'd worked with

00:05:57.149 --> 00:06:00.816
for a decade to leave,
I had to fire people.

00:06:02.901 --> 00:06:06.626
That was the most difficult,
the most difficult thing

00:06:06.626 --> 00:06:08.942
I had to do in business, asking,

00:06:08.942 --> 00:06:11.775
a bunch of people to leave Oracle.

00:06:13.304 --> 00:06:16.338
Our goal was to get, we
started with four employees,

00:06:16.338 --> 00:06:19.155
and our goal was to get to 50 employees,

00:06:19.155 --> 00:06:22.569
and the goal really was not so
much to make a lot of money,

00:06:22.569 --> 00:06:25.250
I wanted to, to me a lot
of money in those days

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:28.420
was I wanted to own a house,
I want to have a nice car,

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:31.810
and I wanted to work
with interesting people

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:33.737
and do interesting things, and I thought

00:06:33.737 --> 00:06:34.969
if I could control my own environment,

00:06:34.969 --> 00:06:36.689
have a small consulting company,

00:06:36.689 --> 00:06:39.111
and Oracle started out as a
software consulting company.

00:06:39.111 --> 00:06:40.611
We worked for Tandem Computer if anyone

00:06:40.611 --> 00:06:42.569
remembers those guys, we worked for IBM,

00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:46.047
we worked for a lot of
different tech companies

00:06:46.047 --> 00:06:48.908
in Silicon Valley doing
contract programming,

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:50.534
fixed price contract programming,

00:06:50.534 --> 00:06:52.734
and we did pretty well, actually.

00:06:52.734 --> 00:06:55.944
Again, the idea was just
to have a small software,

00:06:55.944 --> 00:06:58.111
contract software company.

00:06:59.453 --> 00:07:01.684
We thought we were hotshot programmers,

00:07:01.684 --> 00:07:03.477
we thought we could make a decent living

00:07:03.477 --> 00:07:05.343
doing just that, and quite by accident,

00:07:05.343 --> 00:07:06.602
we built the product.

00:07:06.602 --> 00:07:08.092
My sister once said to me that,

00:07:08.092 --> 00:07:09.857
whenever I got too close
to one of my goals,

00:07:09.857 --> 00:07:13.050
I immediately moved the
goal posts further away.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.645
I think in business
you can always do more.

00:07:15.645 --> 00:07:17.943
- Hi, I'm Rolfe Winkler from
the Wall Street Journal,

00:07:17.943 --> 00:07:21.842
I had a question about your apps business.

00:07:21.842 --> 00:07:23.625
I guess it's, you've built a phenomenal,

00:07:23.625 --> 00:07:25.616
that generates phenomenal cash flows,

00:07:25.616 --> 00:07:27.851
and a lot of people, it's done so a lot

00:07:27.851 --> 00:07:31.344
by locking in customers to older software,

00:07:31.344 --> 00:07:35.511
and raising maintenance
fees a lot year after year,

00:07:36.437 --> 00:07:37.270
- That's not true.

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:38.103
Wait, wait, wait.

00:07:38.103 --> 00:07:38.936
Let me just stop you.

00:07:38.936 --> 00:07:41.320
We do not raise maintenance fees a lot

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:43.212
year after year after year.

00:07:43.212 --> 00:07:44.675
People buy more software,

00:07:44.675 --> 00:07:47.137
and they pay the same
maintenece fees they always pay.

00:07:47.137 --> 00:07:47.970
But go ahead.

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:49.142
- I guess the point I'm,

00:07:49.142 --> 00:07:51.418
Marc Andreessen for
instance, famously said that,

00:07:51.418 --> 00:07:53.242
none of the start ups that work with

00:07:53.242 --> 00:07:55.156
Andreessen Horowitz run Oracle.

00:07:55.156 --> 00:07:57.165
And I guess I'm wondering over time--

00:07:57.165 --> 00:08:00.456
- I know, I know, we just have
to rely on General Electric.

00:08:00.456 --> 00:08:03.683
And on AT&amp;T, and Verizon, rather than--

00:08:03.683 --> 00:08:05.001
- I'm saying it's--

00:08:05.001 --> 00:08:08.889
- Which of the Andreessen
Horowitz companies did we lost?

00:08:08.889 --> 00:08:11.426
Anyway, anyway, they run,
by the way, they run...

00:08:11.426 --> 00:08:13.928
- The next generation of
companies isn't going to run

00:08:13.928 --> 00:08:16.650
Oracle, does that end up causing your

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:18.438
business to shrink dramatically over time?

00:08:18.438 --> 00:08:20.795
- Well, again, we, no.

00:08:20.795 --> 00:08:22.867
Our business isn't
shrinking, it's growing,

00:08:22.867 --> 00:08:24.086
and it's growing rapidly.

00:08:24.086 --> 00:08:28.858
And we, and we have all of
our applications in the Cloud.

00:08:28.858 --> 00:08:30.373
Starting June 6th.

00:08:30.373 --> 00:08:32.762
It took us six, seven, years
to rewrite all this stuff

00:08:32.762 --> 00:08:34.951
for the Cloud, okay?

00:08:34.951 --> 00:08:38.136
Our major competitor,
our major competitor,

00:08:38.136 --> 00:08:39.719
SAP didn't do that.

00:08:41.375 --> 00:08:44.074
So if you look at the market in total,

00:08:44.074 --> 00:08:47.145
you know, you have two
very large companies.

00:08:47.145 --> 00:08:49.357
SAP and Oracle, in applications.

00:08:49.357 --> 00:08:50.518
And then a bunch of smaller--

00:08:50.518 --> 00:08:53.060
- [Rolfe] Salesforce and
Workday, what about those guys?

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.622
- Well it's going to be very interesting

00:08:54.622 --> 00:08:56.352
to monitor Workday.

00:08:56.352 --> 00:08:58.198
We compete with Workday
all the time, all the time.

00:08:58.198 --> 00:08:59.862
And we beat Workday all the time.

00:08:59.862 --> 00:09:01.364
So, in the Cloud.

00:09:01.364 --> 00:09:03.560
Our Fusion HcM has been
out for a while, again

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.441
these are much smaller companies.

00:09:05.441 --> 00:09:08.441
But we are fully in the
Cloud come June 6th,

00:09:08.441 --> 00:09:10.676
with a fully integrated suite.

00:09:10.676 --> 00:09:14.515
So, I expect, you know,
Salesforce will continue to grow.

00:09:14.515 --> 00:09:18.323
I expect we will be bigger,
we're around Pure Sass,

00:09:18.323 --> 00:09:20.680
in Pure Sass, no hosting,
nothing else but Pure Sass,

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:24.129
we're around one billion right
now, we're round numbers,

00:09:24.129 --> 00:09:25.488
but about a billion.

00:09:25.488 --> 00:09:30.039
But SAP, everyone, I mean
what are they going to do?

00:09:30.039 --> 00:09:32.956
If we take shares from SAP, or ERP,

00:09:35.041 --> 00:09:37.620
Salesforce doesn't do ERP.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.465
Workday has got very very
primitive, and I'll at

00:09:40.465 --> 00:09:42.231
least give you a couple words on Workday.

00:09:42.231 --> 00:09:45.217
They don't use a database,
they don't use a database,

00:09:45.217 --> 00:09:47.147
they use an object storage
so they can't really do

00:09:47.147 --> 00:09:51.314
reporting, and they use
Flash as a user interface,

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:56.252
so they can't run on iPhones or iPads.

00:09:56.252 --> 00:09:58.076
Besides that, they're great.

00:09:58.076 --> 00:09:59.421
(audience laughing)

00:09:59.421 --> 00:10:01.761
- All right, on that note, over here.

00:10:01.761 --> 00:10:03.481
- So I'm not worried about Workday,

00:10:03.481 --> 00:10:07.328
I do think Salesforce has
built a franchise over

00:10:07.328 --> 00:10:08.445
a long period of time,

00:10:08.445 --> 00:10:11.411
and they're going to be a formidable
competitor in their space.

00:10:11.411 --> 00:10:13.158
I don't think Workday is.

00:10:13.158 --> 00:10:16.246
Whenever we have a head to
head with Workday in the Cloud,

00:10:16.246 --> 00:10:18.499
our human resources versus
their human resources

00:10:18.499 --> 00:10:20.249
we almost always win.

00:10:21.709 --> 00:10:23.985
And that's before we were fully launched.

00:10:23.985 --> 00:10:25.146
So I'm not worried about them,

00:10:25.146 --> 00:10:27.055
their product's too immature and

00:10:27.055 --> 00:10:28.983
they're too small and frail.

00:10:28.983 --> 00:10:33.576
SAP, and they made horrible
architectural mistakes.

00:10:33.576 --> 00:10:35.409
Horrible, no database.

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:39.873
Marc, Marc uses a really good database.

00:10:39.873 --> 00:10:42.433
Salesforce uses a really good database.

00:10:42.433 --> 00:10:43.838
They use Oracle.

00:10:43.838 --> 00:10:45.666
The NetSuite use a really good database.

00:10:45.666 --> 00:10:47.048
Amazingly good database.

00:10:47.048 --> 00:10:48.348
They use Oracle.

00:10:48.348 --> 00:10:49.421
And they used really good middleware.

00:10:49.421 --> 00:10:50.254
You know what they use?

00:10:50.254 --> 00:10:51.149
They used Oracle.

00:10:51.149 --> 00:10:52.189
(audience laughing)

00:10:52.189 --> 00:10:53.022
- All right, all right.

00:10:53.022 --> 00:10:53.855
We're going to get to the next question.

00:10:53.855 --> 00:10:55.237
- Workday.

00:10:55.237 --> 00:10:57.552
(audience cheering and laughing)

00:10:57.552 --> 00:11:00.552
So your question was are we a loser?

00:11:01.389 --> 00:11:02.222
No!

00:11:02.222 --> 00:11:03.568
(audience cheering)

00:11:03.568 --> 00:11:04.568
- Fantastic.

00:11:06.166 --> 00:11:07.273
Rolfe?

00:11:07.273 --> 00:11:08.106
Rolfe,

00:11:09.613 --> 00:11:11.028
that's how it's done.

00:11:11.028 --> 00:11:12.146
- I think most of the guys,

00:11:12.146 --> 00:11:15.594
the only guys I have trouble
with are the Google guys.

00:11:15.594 --> 00:11:16.650
- Really?

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:20.150
So Larry and Sergey you have trouble with?

00:11:21.023 --> 00:11:22.773
- Larry specifically.

00:11:23.741 --> 00:11:24.741
I, I think--

00:11:26.338 --> 00:11:27.454
- Larry per say?

00:11:27.454 --> 00:11:28.287
- Yeah, Larry per say.

00:11:28.287 --> 00:11:29.120
- Why?

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:30.532
- Because he makes the
decisions over there.

00:11:30.532 --> 00:11:31.727
He runs, he runs that company.

00:11:31.727 --> 00:11:33.266
No one else runs that company.

00:11:33.266 --> 00:11:35.528
And they decided, well
let me be very clear,

00:11:35.528 --> 00:11:37.858
when you do program,
when you write a program

00:11:37.858 --> 00:11:39.878
for the Android phone, you write it,

00:11:39.878 --> 00:11:41.639
you use the Oracle tools,

